---
layout: layouts/base.njk
permalink: /
---
{#
  Rendered from `model/naomi.json` via `_data/matrix.js`. The prose in the
  closing sections is still the site's own and remains provisional.
#}
<main class="page">
  {#
    The standfirst, and the one block competing with the index for the fold.
    Three sentences of it: what the model is, why the levels measure
    assurance, and what a level entitles you to. Keep it this short.
  #}
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="intro-heading">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="intro-heading">Introduction</h2>
    <div class="intro">
      <p>
        A maturity model: five pillars across, five levels down. Each cell is
        an observable practice state — what a team does, not what it intends.
      </p>
      <p>
        Agents make production cheap; checking the result does not get cheaper
        with it. So the levels measure assurance.
        <em class="intro-gloss">{{ matrix.spine.plainGloss }}</em>
        They run <span class="intro-ladder">{{ matrix.spine.shortForm }}</span>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each level states what it entitles you to delegate: more assurance,
        more delegation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="index-heading">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="index-heading">The index</h2>
    {#
      The disclaimer sits over the index rather than in the standfirst because
      it is about the cells specifically — the structure is steady, the content
      in the boxes is what is still being tested. The sentence is the model's
      own, so nothing here can disagree with it about how finished the cells
      are. The printed sheet carries the status chip but not this note.
    #}
    <p class="provisional">
      <span class="provisional-label">{{ matrix.status.label }}</span>
      {{ matrix.status.note }}
    </p>
    {% include "partials/matrix.njk" %}
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="read-heading">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="read-heading">How to read it</h2>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>
        Read down a column to place a capability: find the description that is
        honestly true today, not the one you intend to be true by Christmas. Read
        across a row to see what a level costs everywhere else — levels are
        rarely even, and the uneven ones are the interesting ones.
      </p>
      <p>
        A level is only claimed when it holds without a named individual holding
        it up. If the honest answer is “one person remembers to do this”, that is
        the level below.
      </p>
      <p>
        The index is descriptive, not a target. The top level is not automatically
        the right place to be; it is what the right place looks like when it is
        where you need to be.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="markers-heading">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="markers-heading">AI Native</h2>
    <div class="markers">
      <p class="markers-heading">
        Markers observed in an AI Native organisation:
      </p>
      <ul class="markers-list">
        {% for m in matrix.markers %}<li>{{ m }}</li>{% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="background-heading">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="background-heading">Background</h2>
    <a class="callout" href="{{ site.article.href }}">
      <span class="callout-kicker">The introduction</span>
      <span class="callout-title">{{ site.article.title }}</span>
      {% if site.article.note %}<span class="callout-note">{{ site.article.note }}</span>{% endif %}
      <span class="callout-meta">jasonduffett.net</span>
    </a>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="download-heading">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="download-heading">Download</h2>
    {#
      `sheet` is derived from the model's version in _data/sheet.js, so the
      card cannot point at a stale file or quote a stale size. It is null
      until a sheet has been generated for this version, which is what the
      disabled state is for.
    #}
    {% set downloadLabel = "The printable index" %}
    {% if sheet %}
    <a class="download" href="{{ sheet.path | rel }}" download>
      <span class="download-label">{{ downloadLabel }}</span>
      <span class="download-meta">PDF · A3 · {{ sheet.size }}</span>
    </a>
    {% else %}
    <p class="download download--pending" aria-disabled="true">
      <span class="download-label">{{ downloadLabel }}</span>
      <span class="download-meta">PDF · not published yet</span>
    </p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="links-heading">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="links-heading">Elsewhere</h2>
    <ul class="links">
      {% for link in site.links %}
      <li>
        <a href="{{ link.href }}">{{ link.label }}</a>
        {% if link.note %}<span class="link-note">{{ link.note }}</span>{% endif %}
      </li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
</main>

<script src="{{ '/assets/matrix.js' | rel }}" defer></script>
